1. Global typography and capitalization

Main rule

No ordinary interface text should ever be displayed in all uppercase.

Additionally, use proper sentence case for user-facing interface labels, button names, menu titles, headings, status descriptions, and other UI copy.

Use sentence case instead of title case for multiword interface phrases.

Examples:

Incorrect	Correct
ROSTER	Roster
STAGES	Stages
SELECT FIGHTER	Select fighter
CHANGE STAGE	Change stage
Change Stage	Change stage
Change Fighter	Change fighter
Change CPU	Change CPU
Restart Battle	Restart battle
Return to Home	Return to home
Keep Playing	Keep playing
Random Stage	Random stage
Quick Battle	Quick battle
Watch Mode	Watch mode
Practice Ground	Practice ground
Player 1 Wins	Player 1 wins
CPU Wins	CPU wins
HOLD	Hold

These examples illustrate the required capitalization rules, not an exhaustive list.

Explicit exceptions

The following text may retain its intended uppercase formatting:

* ALVA — all ALVA branding, logos, and wordmarks.
* CPU — including CPU 1 and CPU 2.
* P1 — the player identifier.
* Round and combat announcements — including dramatic battle banners such as ROUND 1, FIGHT, READY, K.O., TIME OVER, and other text that belongs to the same round-announcement system.

Do not extend these exceptions to unrelated interface headings or ordinary status labels.

For example, a dramatic ROUND 1 animation may retain its uppercase presentation, but an ordinary informational label should use Round 1.

Proper names and technical notation

Preserve the established spelling and capitalization of actual proper nouns, fighter names, ability names, and stage names when their capitalization is part of their identity.

Preserve meaningful controller input notation and button glyphs rather than corrupting control instructions.

Do not mechanically lowercase every letter after the first character. Apply the rules intelligently and grammatically.

