2. Fix unintended hover effects and preselected buttons

Problem

Buttons throughout ALVA can appear hovered, focused, or visually selected even when the player has not intentionally interacted with them.

This must be corrected globally.

Required behavior

A button’s mouse-hover appearance must activate only when the mouse pointer is physically over that button.

The hover appearance must disappear immediately when the pointer leaves.

A button must never look hovered simply because:

* A screen was opened.
* A dialogue appeared.
* The button is the default navigation target.
* A previous button retained focus.
* The user clicked the button earlier.
* A keyboard or gamepad navigation function assigned DOM focus.
* The mouse previously occupied that position on another screen.
* A dialogue closed and restored focus to its opener.

Inspect menu navigation

Pay particular attention to:

* js/core/menu-navigator.js
* js/core/screen-manager.js
* js/ui/controller-cursor.js
* js/screens/home-screen.js
* js/screens/map-select-screen.js
* js/screens/mode-select-screen.js

The existing navigation system uses focusFirst(), focusDefault(), and programmatic .focus() calls.

The navigator also focuses elements during mouse pointer interactions.

These behaviors currently interact with CSS selectors such as:

.btn:focus
.home-action:focus
.mode-card:focus
.settings-choice:focus

Audit every similar selector.

Separate four different visual states

Implement a consistent distinction between:

Default state: The normal resting appearance, with no accidental hover styling.

Mouse-hover state: A temporary appearance shown only while the actual pointer is over the button.

Keyboard/gamepad focus state: A separate, accessible focus indicator that shows which control keyboard or gamepad navigation will activate. It must not be mistaken for a mouse-hover fill.

Selected or active state: A persistent state only when the control genuinely represents a selected fighter, stage, language, setting, active tab, or similar value.

Do not confuse selected state with hovered state.

An option may remain visibly selected because it is the user’s saved choice, but it should not simultaneously display a misleading hover effect.

Input modality

Implement reliable mouse, keyboard, gamepad, and touch input-modality handling.

Programmatic focus alone must not trigger mouse-hover visuals.

Default focus may remain internally available for keyboard and controller navigation, but it should not cause unexplained visual highlighting on screen entry.

Keyboard and gamepad users must still have an accessible, clearly visible navigation indicator when using those input methods.

The controller’s circular cursor should produce a hover-equivalent state only when it is actually positioned over the relevant interactive control.

Review .is-cursor-target and remove any stale cursor-hover styling when the cursor moves away, hides, changes scope, or a dialogue closes.

Edge cases

Verify:

1. Opening Home does not create unwanted hover effects.
2. Opening Settings does not make the first tab appear mouse-hovered.
3. Opening a confirmation dialogue does not create unintended button highlighting.
4. Closing a dialogue does not leave its opener visually hovered.
5. Switching screens clears stale hover styling.
6. Moving the mouse away restores the default appearance.
7. Touch input does not leave sticky hover states.
8. Keyboard/gamepad navigation remains functional and accessible.
9. Disabled buttons never respond with hover styling.
10. Actual selected settings, fighters, and stages remain correctly distinguishable.

Do not solve this by globally deleting focus indicators, disabling keyboard navigation, or removing the existing controller navigation system.

